# Task 3 - the same points, read again and again.
#
# k-means repeats one step until it settles. Every round assigns each point to
# its nearest centroid, then recomputes each centroid from the points that
# chose it. The assignment is per point and needs no coordination; the
# recomputation is per centroid and needs all of them, so it is a shuffle.
#
# The two rounds below are written out one after the other on purpose. That is
# what an iterative job is: the same pass over the same data, again. Reading
# `points` off disk on every round is what makes MapReduce a poor fit here, and
# `cache` is the one line that changes it — the points are kept in memory and
# every later round starts from what is already there.
#
# Try these in order. Each step is marked on the line it belongs to.
#
#   1. run it as it is, and count the stages. Which of them had to wait for
#      all the machines, and which did not
#   2. take the cache away, and say what a third round would now cost
#   3. lose the cached points and watch the lineage rebuild them. Note what
#      it did *not* do: it did not read them off disk again
#   4. add a third round by copying the two lines of round two. The pipeline
#      grows by one stage per round, which is the shape of the problem
#   5. make one executor unreliable and run it a few times. Compare what a
#      lost partition costs here against a lost mapper in Task 1

# --- the machines ----------------------------------------------------

@machine
class Executor:
    pass

e1 = Executor(speed=1.0)
# step 5: add   error_rate=0.3, on_crash="restart", restart_after=0.5
e2 = Executor(speed=1.0)
e3 = Executor(speed=1.0)

# --- the world -------------------------------------------------------

world = World(machines=[e1, e2, e3])

# --- the pipeline ----------------------------------------------------

points = textFile("points.csv")
kept = points.cache()          # step 2: make this   kept = points.map(value)

# round one: assign every point to a centroid, then recompute the centroids
nearest1 = kept.mapToPair(value, 1)
centroids1 = nearest1.reduceByKey(a + b)

# round two: the same pass, over the same points
# step 4: copy these two lines for a third round, and rename them
nearest2 = kept.mapToPair(value, 1)
centroids2 = nearest2.reduceByKey(a + b)

job = Spark(pipeline=centroids2, lose=kept)

world.run(job)
